obs THE REBASE COST ME A NUMBER, A FOLD, AND A CITATION HABIT -- ALL THREE TO ONE PEER COMMIT. Upstream landed 0877a5f56 while I was committing: it booked its OWN %513 at 20260906.210117, folded %489 together with %508 and %509 to one shelf, and seated a law with a guard behind it. Mine were unshared, so mine gave way three times.
obs My %513 renumbered to %514 and my %514 to %515; the stamps 20260906.211015 and 20260906.211529 never moved, which is the derived spine working exactly as written. The closed row's shelf was renamed rows-513 to rows-514 in the same breath, since a shelf filename spells the number too.
obs My %489 fold was WITHDRAWN WHOLE rather than merged. A peer shelved %489 with %508 and %509 under one lesson and published first; two shelves for one row is worse than either shelf alone, so mine was deleted and the recital line with it. That is the derived spine's rule applied to a fold instead of a number -- and it is the third time in two laps that this seat has given work back to a peer who published first.
obs AND THE LAW THAT LANDED IS ABOUT THE HABIT I WAS EXERCISING WHILE IT LANDED. Rule 4 of derived-spine -- cite by stamp until the row is shared -- now has a gate, unshared_citation_scan.sh, reading shared_max off the spine and holding citations above it at zero on living surfaces. My card block and my cairn both cited %513 and %514 by number. Both now cite by stamp; the scan reads unshared_citations=0. The ledger and dated testimony are exempt by its own written reasons, so this log keeps its numbers.
obs The pin arithmetic moved with the withdrawal: one fold rather than two, 37,317 with 3,643 of headroom rather than 38,498. The row was corrected to the measured number rather than left reciting the one it was written with.
obs And %440 fired on this rebase exactly where the last three laps said it would -- upstream's 210117 row auto-merged above my 212202 one and broke newest-first, rows_misordered=1. One sort by stamp descending returned it to green. That is its thirteenth firing across the fleet by the running count, and the tenth to land on a shelf after a rebase rather than before it.
